On the JavaScript/HTML target, a function can read and forward a Wire, form a
Route and forward it to another function. HTML-Wasm supports Wires and
reports a target error if reachable code forms a Route. The examples on
this page use the branch's accepted brace syntax. The current Alpha parser
still uses the earlier declaration and block syntax.

A builder that offered an interactive row editor would need to read the Wire,
listen for an edit, use the Route to make a message and deliver that message
to the application. Moth doesn't currently supply that event handling.
Returning a String from `row_label` gives the caller the text from that call.
Changing `title` later won't update an already displayed label.

When your program already has a complete message, handle it with ordinary
code. This example uses the choice from the previous section and prints
`Row 42: Buy milk`.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    describe |message TodoMessage| -> String {
        return if message is {
            case Rename(id, text): then [:Row [id]: [text]]
            case Delete(id): then [:Delete row [id]]
        }
    }

    io.line(describe(TodoMessage::Rename(42, "Buy milk")))
]

To pass a completed message between tasks, Moth's accepted design uses a
channel. Channel execution still awaits compiler and runtime support. See
@../async (Channels and readiness) and @../progress (implementation status).
